n and so it becomes a big  national political question and as you  know in our current moment that means it  gets a jaggedness that wasn't there  before yeah let's talk about the  executive branch John what's the what's  the mark of an effective leader during  these times  well there is kind of a playbook and you  see a lot of leaders across the world  and governor's benefiting from doing  three basic things they tell the truth  even if the truth is kind of brutal when  I've interviewed experts about these  disasters they say tell the people the  truth because even if it's hard people  appreciate that they also take  responsibility because that aligns  everybody in the same direction it tells  a leader tells their staff you can't  explain your way out of this don't spin  your way out of this you need results  and then the other thing is they inform  people because even if there is  uncertainty information helps people  feel control and people need to feel  control and certainty to eventually  someday start getting back to a more  normal kind of life if they don't feel  that certainty and that sense of control  then everything that feels heiwa sort of  well out of their control and that means  they're not going to be able to get back  to the to life as they lifted before so  John how does a good leader balance the  gravity of is the situation and trying  to give people optimism we saw stories  of people on the beach saying well the  president doesn't wear a mask why do I  need to wear a mask people are actually  saying that and following his example  well usually what a president does is  focus on two things unity we're all in  this together and also stitches the  current moment into the national story  why do they do that because  psychologists tell us that in these  moments where we are all experiencing  something brand-new and afresh the brain  reacts by wanting to fix it wanting to  flee it operates at a kind of hazardous  level what a president or a leader can  